{
  "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
  "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Ashley C. Keller",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KELLER: Mr. Chief Justice, and\nmay it please the Court:\nAs far back as 1827, states enacted\nlaws like Pennsylvania's, and by 1868 or shortly\nthereafter, every state in the union had at\nleast one and often several\nconsent-by-registration statutes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ashley C. Keller",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That approach is not originalism.\nIt's paying lip service to originalism, treating\nthe doctrine as an infinitely malleable pretext\nto reach a policy outcome that Norfolk Southern\nand other big businesses prefer."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Mr. Keller, if we\nwere to look through history and only find a\nhandful of states that had laws similar to\nPennsylvania's, how would we know when there's\nenough history to support your position?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ashley C. Keller",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KELLER: No, Justice Jackson, I don't think it's too simplistic. The reason I gravitate towards the word \"consent\" is that's historically how courts referred to it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Why should we distinguish between the two? Why -- why is that even a relevant consideration?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Mr. Keller, I'd like to take you back to a question that Justice Thomas asked very early about do you recognize a kind of preexisting right here, pre-waiver, that is."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: I thought --I thought the Court's precedents made clear that the state does not have the right any longer to exclude out-of-state businesses from that state's market."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: -- that cut the Gordian knot?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: If -- that -- if excluding a foreign corporation from the state would violate the dormant Commerce Clause, can you prevail?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Professor Sachs",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0001-0008",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The negative Commerce Clause precedent in this area is muddied. Professor Sachs talks about this in a cogent way in his amicus submission."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Keller",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think that I then said the state has a sovereign interest in treating all people equally and not discriminating against out-of-state residents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Keller",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I am not suggesting that we overrule Daimler and Goodyear. They're -- only my friend is asking to overturn precedent today. We are not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Keller",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And I don't think that it would actually gut those opinions. It would definitely change the jurisdictional landscape, but I think big businesses, in particular, the ones that are national in scope, they are capable of making choices about what they're going to do in the wake of rules that are embraced by policymakers."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0002-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Only Pennsylvania has a statute like this, correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Keller",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I wouldn't call it \"at home.\" I would say they would make a choice whether to consent to do business in the state and, therefore, be subject to general jurisdiction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Keller",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That is. And to make sure that I stated it correctly, I could win under the -- the Due Process Clause, which is the question presented here, excepting the premise that they're not allowed to do this"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Phillips",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The only basis on which the plaintiff argues you should ignore the precedent in Daimler is because there was a sheet of paper filled out by which my client registered to do business."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Phillips",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "fill out a registration in order to be able to do business in the state. Once having done that, you have then consented to general jurisdiction in all cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Phillips",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0002-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And, indeed, if you look at the Pennsylvania statute, the Pennsylvania statute says that you can be sued in Pennsylvania for consent to the extent of your consent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 71,
        "start": 71
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: I -- I'm sorry, counselor. I -- I -- I think I'm now finally understanding. You don't accept the SG's position that -- that takes -- well, I think you admitted that historical case law permits consent-by-registration to cases arising from doing business in the state, correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 88,
        "start": 88
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "My question was, how? Daimler International -- Daimler was a non-consent case. All of the cases that you rely upon for specific jurisdiction and general jurisdiction are all non-consent statute cases. So how can we say that overrules Pennsylvania?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Phillips",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0004-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 88,
        "start": 88
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, what the Court -- I mean, what the Court said in Daimler, and as repeated in other cases, is that all of the -- all of the precedents between Pennoyer and International Shoe, all of them, that ultimately rely on some basic concept of territoriality --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0004-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 91,
        "start": 91
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Sure I understand where we're at. Consent lives on after International Shoe, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0004-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 92,
        "start": 92
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Just to follow up on Justice Gorsuch's question, when he says, consent lives on, you mean, if you're sued in Philadelphia and you show up and say I have a right not to be sued here, but, nonetheless, I'm going to let it go --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Curtis E. Gannon",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0004-0006",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 102,
        "start": 102
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF CURTIS E. GANNON FOR THE UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE, SUPPORTING THE RESPONDENT MR. GANNON: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Petitioner's theory of general jurisdiction on the basis of supposed consent is inconsistent with all of this Court's recent cases about general and specific jurisdiction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0004-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 109,
        "start": 109
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Mr. Gannon, so I just have three -- three questions. First, the way I understand it, you're saying this is coerced consent and, therefore, isn't real consent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gannon",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0005-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 110,
        "start": 110
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I think it will depend on how coercive the condition is. And so, under the unconstitutional conditions doctrine, as the Court summarized in its Koontz opinion, there needs to be a nexus and rough proportionality between the condition that's being imposed and the right that's being waived."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0005-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 111,
        "start": 111
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That leads me to my third question. Let's suppose this were a foreign squared case rather than a foreign cubed case and -- and so Pennsylvania had a resident who had been injured. Would the consent here"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0005-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 114,
        "start": 114
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Now you have a different argument that either the dormant Commerce Clause, I think, or the unconstitutional conditions doctrine stop a state from saying to a corporation what?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gannon",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0005-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 116,
        "start": 116
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But we think that consent needs to be an actual choice. And when it's -- when it's done with this coercion, it's not. If -- if and -- but that doesn't mean that it can't be done ex ante."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gannon",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0005-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 118,
        "start": 118
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I agree that that particular form of leverage isn't happening here, but our basic point is that when we're talking about a foreign cubed situation, there isn't a sufficient interest in the State of Pennsylvania in this lawsuit in order for it to justify the waiver of the right of the defendant not to be haled into this Court when there aren't sufficient ties to otherwise justify the dispute being resolved there."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0005-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 119,
        "start": 119
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "More business than it does in its state of registration or where it incorporated, correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gannon",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "17c21e7f79a06e3278d9174eccc41e891f7dcabb21978c156178e9e3dfaf0e58",
      "evidence_id": "ev-17c21e7f79a0-0005-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 121,
        "start": 121
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In the \"Interest of the United States\" section of our brief, we pointed out not just that the excessive rules of -- of general -- the excessive availability of general jurisdiction could cause international concerns for trade with the United States and our commercial interests, but also the Petitioner had called into question the constitutionality of a federal statute, and so we thought that it was important to make sure that the Court's decision here wouldn't implicate the constitutionality of federal statutes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Keller",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1168",
